1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,200
So originally, the plan was to talk more

2
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:09,660
about reinforcement learning.

3
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:12,340
But I thought since I only had one lecture to give,

4
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,640
I think this class would be a little bit

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:17,780
incomplete without saying something about multimodality,

6
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:21,880
which is so pervasive if you look at all the major models.

7
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:27,040
So this will be an overview of multimodal models.

8
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,960
Of course, this could be a whole class in itself,

9
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,759
and I'll try to do what I can in one lecture.

10
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:35,500
OK, so let's dive into multimodal models.

11
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:38,280
So far in this class, we have exclusively

12
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:40,240
focused on language models.

13
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,300
And language models are already pretty general.

14
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:45,760
They give you the ability to go from any piece of text

15
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:47,180
to any text.

16
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:53,340
So this could be any language, code, poetry, anything you like,

17
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:57,680
even, let's say, DNA, for example.

18
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,380
But the world is multimodal.

19
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:05,260
You have text, you have images, audio, video.

20
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:09,820
And if you think about a North Star of where we want to be

21
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:12,580
is what people call omni model, which

22
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:17,220
is ability to take any combination of these modalities

23
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:20,860
and output any combination of any of these modalities.

24
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:24,500
So, for example, I can give you an image and a video

25
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:29,920
and I can ask a question about the image and the video.

26
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:31,080
I can generate images.

27
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:34,400
I can convert the audio into an image.

28
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,140
You can imagine all sorts of different applications

29
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,000
by having an omni model.

30
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,620
So in this class, I'm not going to build up

31
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:42,920
to a whole omni model.

32
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:47,900
But I'll give you some of the pieces that at least allow

33
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:52,900
you to ingest a little bit of images, which we'll mostly

34
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:55,900
focus on today.

35
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:57,990
So how do you build such a thing?

36
00:01:57,990 --> 00:02:02,210
Well, this is a weird way to motivate this,

37
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:06,070
but the reality is that transformers work really well.

38
00:02:06,070 --> 00:02:10,630
And despite the best efforts of people to try other things,

39
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:14,410
those are across all of these modalities still at scale

40
00:02:14,410 --> 00:02:15,690
the best thing we have.

41
00:02:15,690 --> 00:02:17,910
所以我们必须弄清楚如何使用它们。

42
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:21,190
而如今，Transformer 是为文本设计的。

43
00:02:21,190 --> 00:02:24,910
所以它们具有这样的特性：它们使用 token。

44
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:27,990
它们接收一堆 token 作为输入，

45
00:02:27,990 --> 00:02:30,310
然后输出一些 token。

46
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:34,070
在这里，我扩展了

47
00:02:34,070 --> 00:02:36,950
tokenization 的概念，使其不只是我们

48
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:40,730
在文本中见过的离散 token，还可以是连续的 token。

49
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:44,830
你可以把它们看作 token 的嵌入。

50
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:46,750
关键在于，一个 token

51
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:51,350
应该表示某种语义单元的信息。

52
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:55,390
例如，在自然语言中，token 是 subword。

53
00:02:55,390 --> 00:02:58,050
它们有一定意义。

54
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:02,090
而例如，一个像素本身当然不是

55
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:04,890
有意义的。

56
00:03:04,890 --> 00:03:07,430
所以我们必须以某种方式把所有东西，

57
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:13,530
包括音频和图像，转换成离散或连续的

58
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:15,370
token。

59
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:18,450
现在请注意，即使是文本，我们也已经不得不这样做了。

60
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:21,570
在第一讲中，我们讨论了 tokenization。

61
00:03:21,570 --> 00:03:24,830
在那里，这还不算太糟。

62
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:26,750
我的意思是，我们有一个 BP tokenizer。

63
00:03:26,750 --> 00:03:30,010
你们实现了它。

64
00:03:30,010 --> 00:03:31,210
这没问题。

65
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:37,690
人们可能会希望有更好的 tokenizer，或者某种

66
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:39,590
根本不需要 tokenizer 的东西。

67
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:43,810
但这也不是世界上最糟糕的事情。

68
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:46,530
现在，对于非文本模态，我们

69
00:03:46,530 --> 00:03:49,410
需要更加绞尽脑汁，弄清楚

70
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:51,810
BP tokenizer 的等价物是什么，它

71
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:54,020
能接收一张图像并生成

72
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:57,340
Transformer 可以消化的东西。

73
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:01,280
好，所以这里你必须回答两个问题。

74
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,460
一是，对于非文本数据，例如图像或视频

75
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:09,700
或音频，如何将它们输入到 Transformer 中？

76
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:12,740
然后如何将它们生成出来？

77
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:17,579
本讲将主要关注第一个问题。

78
00:04:17,579 --> 00:04:25,860
好，那么让我们回到过去，把时间倒回到 21 年。

79
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:28,980
所以 CLIP 模型，你们中一些人可能熟悉，

80
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:34,260
是现代视觉语言模型的许多基础。

81
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:39,780
And so we can go through this a bit in more detail.

82
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:43,500
So CLIP stands for Contrastive Language Image Pre-Training.

83
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:46,460
And a bit of historical context is around

84
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:52,040
that time GPT-3 had already happened.

85
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:52,840
GPT-2.

86
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,480
All the language models had already

87
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:59,200
gone into this foundation model era.

88
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,220
Neovision was still-- there were some efforts,

89
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:08,980
but traditionally had been based on large annotated data

90
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:12,760
sets, such as ImageNet, and training models,

91
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:15,920
such as the ResNet, on these data sets

92
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:19,160
and doing a bunch of other things, like data augmentation,

93
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:21,720
and getting really good performance.

94
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:25,960
And so the researchers at OpenAI were wondering,

95
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:29,520
is it possible to leverage the large amount of image

96
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:32,480
and textual captions out there?

97
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,840
So inspired by this idea in language where you could just

98
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:39,460
scrape the internet, you have all this text, it's very noisy.

99
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:42,180
But somehow if you train a large enough model,

100
00:05:42,180 --> 00:05:45,680
you can make sense out of this and generate something useful.

101
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:49,720
So what was the equivalent of that for images?

102
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:53,420
So as a result, CLIP was born.

103
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:58,900
And the idea of CLIP is fairly simple and straightforward.

104
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:02,380
So the idea here is that suppose I give you

105
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:05,980
a bunch of image-text pairs.

106
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:09,220
So let's say I give you 32,000 of these.

107
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:13,140
OK, so what I'm going to do is for each image

108
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:16,900
I'm going to encode it using an image encoder, which I'll

109
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:20,380
explain later, into a vector.

110
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:26,380
So I'm going to have 32,000 of these image encodings.

111
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:27,940
I'm going to also do the same thing

112
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:30,400
with the corresponding text.

113
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:33,960
So that will give me T1 through TN.

114
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:36,240
So these are the embeddings of the text.

115
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:39,100
These are embedding of the images.

116
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:41,220
Now, the objective I want to set up

117
00:06:41,220 --> 00:06:48,550
is that I want the image-- let's take I1.

118
00:06:48,550 --> 00:06:52,410
So I1 is aligned with T1.

119
00:06:52,410 --> 00:06:57,670
So I want this alignment, the dot product between these two

120
00:06:57,670 --> 00:07:01,750
embeddings to be much larger than the dot product between I1

121
00:07:01,750 --> 00:07:04,590
and all the other texts, OK.

122
00:07:04,590 --> 00:07:08,830
And conversely, I also want for this text

123
00:07:08,830 --> 00:07:11,110
to say that this dot product is larger

124
00:07:11,110 --> 00:07:14,590
than the dot product between T1 and all the other images.

125
00:07:14,590 --> 00:07:18,930
So that, in a nutshell, is the CLIP objective.

126
00:07:18,930 --> 00:07:22,270
And you can imagine doing—there's basically

127
00:07:22,270 --> 00:07:30,350
2 times N different softmax classification problems.

128
00:07:30,350 --> 00:07:33,990
So here's the code.

129
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:40,470
So you get an n by d matrix for the image encodings

130
00:07:40,470 --> 00:07:43,870
and n by d for the textual embeddings.

131
00:07:43,870 --> 00:07:48,570
You normalize and you take this dot product

132
00:07:48,570 --> 00:07:50,370
with some temperature.

133
00:07:50,370 --> 00:07:56,970
And then you compute the cross entropy, OK.

134
00:07:56,970 --> 00:08:00,210
So it's basically a multi-class classification problem,

135
00:08:00,210 --> 00:08:09,050
where the examples are structured in this m by n matrix form.

136
00:08:09,050 --> 00:08:11,390
OK, any questions about that?

137
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:24,050
All right, so some more details about how CLIP was built.

138
00:08:24,050 --> 00:08:26,610
So where does the data come from?

139
00:08:26,610 --> 00:08:32,890
There are not too many details in the paper about the data.

140
00:08:32,890 --> 00:08:39,490
Roughly, you take a bunch of queries from somewhere

141
00:08:39,490 --> 00:08:43,250
and then you go search online for these

142
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:47,630
and you mine a bunch of image and text pairs.

143
00:08:47,630 --> 00:08:53,590
And this resulted in 400 million image-text pairs.

144
00:08:53,590 --> 00:08:55,990
The data set wasn't released, but there

145
00:08:55,990 --> 00:09:00,270
was an effort by this paper called

146
00:09:00,270 --> 00:09:09,110
OpenCLIP, which replicated and further extended the CLIP ideas.

147
00:09:09,110 --> 00:09:12,090
So there was a data set called LAION-5B,

148
00:09:12,090 --> 00:09:14,430
which was released 5 billion images

149
00:09:14,430 --> 00:09:16,350
with textual descriptions.

150
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:19,470
And there was a bunch of processing that happened.

151
00:09:19,470 --> 00:09:22,030
Interestingly, they actually used CLIP for the data filtering

152
00:09:22,030 --> 00:09:24,310
and then train OpenCLIP.

153
00:09:24,310 --> 00:09:26,530
So there's some bootstrapping happening.

154
00:09:26,530 --> 00:09:28,390
But at least this is a model where

155
00:09:28,390 --> 00:09:31,350
you can point to the data set that it was trained

156
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:34,290
on and the code as well.

157
00:09:36,950 --> 00:09:41,630
So a few other details.

158
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:45,320
So images come in all sorts of resolutions.

159
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:47,680
If you find an internet image, it

160
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:52,960
might be long and skinny or tall—sorry.

161
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,000
Long and skinny or the other way.

162
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,240
So, arbitrary dimensions.

163
00:09:57,240 --> 00:10:01,800
And one thing that you learn about neural nets

164
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,820
is that they don't like things to be dynamic.

165
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:06,840
They want things to be fixed size.

166
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:11,560
So there is a somewhat heuristic processing

167
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:16,600
that happens is they resize the image so that the shorter side

168
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:20,000
is 336 or whatever target size.

169
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
It could be 224 as well.

170
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,520
And then they center crop to get a square.

171
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:29,660
OK, this is obviously for convenience.

172
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:32,580
Later, we'll see that you can do better than this.

173
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:35,000
But this is just for expediency.

174
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,560
And also at this time the CLIP authors

175
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:42,400
were thinking about ImageNet and classification.

176
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:46,140
So usually, the object is in the middle

177
00:10:46,140 --> 00:10:48,040
and you're just trimming off some background.

178
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:49,760
So it doesn't matter too much.

179
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:57,720
So what is the vision encoder?

180
00:10:57,720 --> 00:10:59,520
So this is the general algorithm.

181
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:05,520
But I haven't specified what the image and text encoders are.

182
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:06,020
Yeah.

183
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:28,100
So the question I think is why they train on image-text pairs

184
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:29,560
as opposed to images.

185
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:36,380
So there is a line of work that tries

186
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:41,110
to encode images into representations

187
00:11:41,110 --> 00:11:43,650
based on data augmentation.

188
00:11:43,650 --> 00:11:46,830
So, for example, if you take an image-- and I think

189
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:48,090
this idea called SimCLR.

190
00:11:48,090 --> 00:11:49,590
You augment it.

191
00:11:49,590 --> 00:11:50,410
You crop it.

192
00:11:50,410 --> 00:11:52,630
You randomly perturb it.

193
00:11:52,630 --> 00:11:53,570
Rotate it.

194
00:11:53,570 --> 00:11:56,850
And you want to say that that is the same image.

195
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:58,610
They should have similar embeddings.

196
00:12:01,496 --> 00:12:05,410
And that's useful for low level details.

197
00:12:05,410 --> 00:12:09,710
But the problem is that you won't data

198
00:12:09,710 --> 00:12:14,390
augment your way from one type of dog to another dog.

199
00:12:14,390 --> 00:12:16,910
So by using the text, it gives you

200
00:12:16,910 --> 00:12:23,830
a higher level semantic representations of images.

201
00:12:23,830 --> 00:12:25,930
And, yeah, that's a good question.

202
00:12:25,930 --> 00:12:29,590
And later, we'll see how these semantic representations are

203
00:12:29,590 --> 00:12:31,510
useful if you look at the types of tasks

204
00:12:31,510 --> 00:12:33,850
that people are trying to solve.

205
00:12:36,870 --> 00:12:41,450
OK, so what is the vision encoder that CLIP uses?

206
00:12:41,450 --> 00:12:44,930
They actually experimented with a bunch of different ones,

207
00:12:44,930 --> 00:12:49,290
ResNets and vision transformers, which had just come out.

208
00:12:49,290 --> 00:12:51,990
They found out that vision transformers performed the best.

209
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:56,770
So when people say CLIP, they usually mean the VIT version.

210
00:12:56,770 --> 00:13:03,130
And so a vision transformer does the following.

211
00:13:03,130 --> 00:13:10,930
So you take an image and you break it up into these patches.

212
00:13:10,930 --> 00:13:15,250
I think the original paper was 16 by 16

213
00:13:15,250 --> 00:13:18,850
and the CLIP used 14 by 14.

214
00:13:18,850 --> 00:13:21,730
But anyway, you have these little patches,

215
00:13:21,730 --> 00:13:24,850
which are basically vectors.

216
00:13:24,850 --> 00:13:27,970
So each patch is in some sense a--

217
00:13:27,970 --> 00:13:31,930
well, a token for this vision transformer.

218
00:13:31,930 --> 00:13:33,490
You add the positional embeddings

219
00:13:33,490 --> 00:13:36,630
just like you would if you were training a language model,

220
00:13:36,630 --> 00:13:42,310
and then you just put it through a standard transformer.

221
00:13:42,310 --> 00:13:50,190
And if you want to classify, then you would--

222
00:13:50,190 --> 00:13:55,230
well, you would usually want to--OK, sorry.

223
00:13:55,230 --> 00:14:03,550
The transformer encoder produces a sequence of vectors.

224
00:14:03,550 --> 00:14:07,530
And the CLIP paper does something a little bit different.

225
00:14:07,530 --> 00:14:08,947
You can average all these vectors.

226
00:14:08,947 --> 00:14:10,350
That gives you a single vector.

227
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:14,230
But they thought it was better to do attention pooling, which

228
00:14:14,230 --> 00:14:17,830
means that you take the global average of all the activations,

229
00:14:17,830 --> 00:14:22,630
but then you also do another round of attention

230
00:14:22,630 --> 00:14:27,590
with that query against the key and values of each position.

231
00:14:27,590 --> 00:14:29,530
And that gives you another vector,

232
00:14:29,530 --> 00:14:32,310
which is maybe a little bit more informed

233
00:14:32,310 --> 00:14:37,080
rather than just a straight up average.

234
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:40,280
OK, so that's the VIT.

235
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:45,720
And the CLIP paper found that the best model is what is called

236
00:14:45,720 --> 00:14:46,320
VIT-L/14.

237
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:54,040
So to decipher, this is a large VIT.

238
00:14:54,040 --> 00:14:58,760
I think it has 24 layers or so.

239
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:00,880
Not quite sure about that.

240
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:03,960
And 14 by 14 patches.

241
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:07,320
Each patch is RGB, so it has three channels.

242
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:13,420
And they trained on 336 by 336 resolution images.

243
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:19,120
Actually, they trained on a lower res

244
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:23,452
I guess for speed because high resolution images take longer.

245
00:15:23,452 --> 00:15:25,160
And then for the latter part of training,

246
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:27,720
they train on higher res.

247
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:29,000
Yeah.

248
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,040
For the position embedding, would it

249
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:33,440
be more complicated than the task?

250
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:36,140
For example, task only has [INAUDIBLE].

251
00:15:40,660 --> 00:15:44,670
Yeah, so the question is, can you

252
00:15:44,670 --> 00:15:46,420
be smarter about the positional embeddings

253
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:51,060
because this is just linear 0 through 9?

254
00:15:51,060 --> 00:15:54,220
I think in this paper, they actually-- in the CLIP paper,

255
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,500
I believe they tried some 2D version of this.

256
00:15:57,500 --> 00:16:02,300
And the one DNA found that it doesn't really matter that much.

257
00:16:02,300 --> 00:16:05,440
I think it's also--

258
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:09,260
I always have to take these results with the idea

259
00:16:09,260 --> 00:16:11,480
that they're interested in classification in mind.

260
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:13,560
So for classification, maybe it doesn't matter.

261
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:16,540
Later, we'll see a fancier positional embeddings

262
00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:20,840
that do take into account the spatial structure.

263
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:30,200
OK, so the text encoder is a standard transformer.

264
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:33,270
It's a GPT-2 style transformer since that

265
00:16:33,270 --> 00:16:37,070
was from the same group that developed GPT-2.

266
00:16:37,070 --> 00:16:42,830
And in order to get a single vector out of a sequence,

267
00:16:42,830 --> 00:16:47,230
they prepended BOS and appended EOS.

268
00:16:47,230 --> 00:16:51,390
And then they took EOS, the activation at the highest layer

269
00:16:51,390 --> 00:16:57,830
as the representation of that whole sequence.

270
00:16:57,830 --> 00:16:59,290
OK, so you have the vision encoder.

271
00:16:59,290 --> 00:17:00,830
You have the text encoder.

272
00:17:00,830 --> 00:17:06,349
And then you can do this process to train.

273
00:17:06,349 --> 00:17:07,369
You pick a batch.

274
00:17:07,369 --> 00:17:08,650
You encode all the text.

275
00:17:08,650 --> 00:17:10,250
You decode all the images.

276
00:17:10,250 --> 00:17:14,990
And then you form these two end cross-entropy losses.

277
00:17:14,990 --> 00:17:18,270
And then you go from there.

278
00:17:18,270 --> 00:17:21,710
OK, so the headline result, which

279
00:17:21,710 --> 00:17:25,910
got a lot of people excited about CLIP back in 2021,

280
00:17:25,910 --> 00:17:28,690
is that on the ImageNet benchmark,

281
00:17:28,690 --> 00:17:32,330
zero-shot CLIP outperforms a ResNet that was trained

282
00:17:32,330 --> 00:17:36,090
on 1.2 million ImageNet images.

283
00:17:36,090 --> 00:17:39,270
So if you think about this, 1.2 million ImageNet images,

284
00:17:39,270 --> 00:17:44,650
this was many, many hours of Amazon Mechanical Turk worker

285
00:17:44,650 --> 00:17:48,490
time to do this annotation.

286
00:17:48,490 --> 00:17:52,890
And now you have CLIP that was trained on more organic web

287
00:17:52,890 --> 00:17:54,410
data.

288
00:17:54,410 --> 00:17:57,030
Of course, this was the labor of a bunch of people on the web.

289
00:17:57,030 --> 00:18:01,730
But if you're able to leverage that or existing data already,

290
00:18:01,730 --> 00:18:04,650
then you can just zero-shot it.

291
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:07,770
And the zero-shot technique is basically

292
00:18:07,770 --> 00:18:10,870
to explain—I don't have slide support here.

293
00:18:10,870 --> 00:18:14,230
But you take an image and then you have a bunch of text.

294
00:18:14,230 --> 00:18:16,130
And then you basically take the dot product

295
00:18:16,130 --> 00:18:19,790
with the various labels and see which one's the highest.

296
00:18:23,630 --> 00:18:24,130
Oh, yeah.

297
00:18:24,130 --> 00:18:24,710
It is.

298
00:18:24,710 --> 00:18:25,250
Sorry.

299
00:18:25,250 --> 00:18:26,930
It is on this part of the slide.

300
00:18:26,930 --> 00:18:28,970
OK, good point.

301
00:18:28,970 --> 00:18:32,850
So this is a zero-shot prediction,

302
00:18:32,850 --> 00:18:35,450
where you take an image and then you—yeah, what I said.

303
00:18:40,870 --> 00:18:41,410
Cool.

304
00:18:41,410 --> 00:18:43,370
Any questions?

305
00:18:43,370 --> 00:18:43,870
Yeah.

306
00:19:00,470 --> 00:19:02,230
Yeah, so the question is if you have a dog

307
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:05,270
and there's maybe other captions that also have dogs,

308
00:19:05,270 --> 00:19:08,670
would that confuse the model?

309
00:19:08,670 --> 00:19:13,430
In general, this process is going to be noisy.

310
00:19:13,430 --> 00:19:18,172
It's OK if there's another dog because, on average, it's

311
00:19:18,172 --> 00:19:20,130
unlikely that there's always going to be a dog.

312
00:19:20,130 --> 00:19:23,950
Sometimes it's going to be an apple or a cat or something.

313
00:19:23,950 --> 00:19:29,040
But also another point is that these images are basically

314
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:34,060
scraped from images on the web with textual text next to them

315
00:19:34,060 --> 00:19:36,860
or in the alt image.

316
00:19:36,860 --> 00:19:38,900
And so they're extremely noisy.

317
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:41,280
There's been studies that look at when

318
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:45,160
you have a caption of an image, it doesn't necessarily just

319
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:48,547
Verbatim say what's in the image.

320
00:19:48,547 --> 00:19:50,380
Because if you have an image and it's a dog,

321
00:19:50,380 --> 00:19:52,760
you don't need to say a dog.

322
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:55,220
So this process is very noisy.

323
00:19:55,220 --> 00:20:02,517
So it's maybe somewhat surprising but also interesting

324
00:20:02,517 --> 00:20:03,100
that it works.

325
00:20:03,100 --> 00:20:06,060
I mean, there was a lot of data filtering that is needed.

326
00:20:06,060 --> 00:20:09,300
So if you take arbitrary images and text on the web,

327
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:12,920
it's probably going to be way too noisy.

328
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:15,840
One other thing to note, which relates

329
00:20:15,840 --> 00:20:18,120
to a point I'll come back to at the very end,

330
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:21,560
is they also try this alternative where

331
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:24,180
instead of doing this ranking, they can

332
00:20:24,180 --> 00:20:26,520
predict a text from the images.

333
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:28,100
So they can set an objective where

334
00:20:28,100 --> 00:20:30,180
they try to take the images and predict

335
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:38,980
the text, either as a bag of words or as a language model.

336
00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:43,780
And they show that maybe, I don't know, somewhat

337
00:20:43,780 --> 00:20:47,080
surprisingly, that if you use a stronger model,

338
00:20:47,080 --> 00:20:49,500
it actually does worse or at least

339
00:20:49,500 --> 00:20:55,980
is less efficient compared to using bag of words, CLIP.

340
00:20:55,980 --> 00:21:00,620
So this is saying that--

341
00:21:00,620 --> 00:21:05,420
I think saying that for actually the representations

342
00:21:05,420 --> 00:21:09,120
that you're trying to learn-- or, again, this ImageNet accuracy.

343
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:10,900
So it's classification.

344
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:15,180
Actually modeling the exact token sequences of the caption

345
00:21:15,180 --> 00:21:20,340
isn't so important for getting the rough representation

346
00:21:20,340 --> 00:21:21,200
of the image.

347
00:21:24,484 --> 00:21:29,670
OK, we'll come back to this point at the end.

348
00:21:29,670 --> 00:21:32,430
So what did we learn so far?

349
00:21:32,430 --> 00:21:35,550
So we've encoded images using CLIP.

350
00:21:35,550 --> 00:21:38,990
And it captures the semantics of the image

351
00:21:38,990 --> 00:21:40,650
because it's paired with text.

352
00:21:40,650 --> 00:21:44,830
And generally, text talks about the semantics.

353
00:21:44,830 --> 00:21:48,030
It is important to note that the design decisions here are

354
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:50,190
based on image classification.

355
00:21:50,190 --> 00:21:53,150
So it's not very fine-grained.

356
00:21:53,150 --> 00:21:56,430
But yet, it's still, as we'll see, serves

357
00:21:56,430 --> 00:21:58,990
as a robust starting point for everything

358
00:21:58,990 --> 00:22:01,350
we're going to do later.

359
00:22:01,350 --> 00:22:07,750
One technical downside of CLIP is that it does require large

360
00:22:07,750 --> 00:22:10,510
batch sizes, like 30,000.

361
00:22:10,510 --> 00:22:12,350
If you have a batch size of 1, clearly, it

362
00:22:12,350 --> 00:22:14,830
doesn't work, or even 10, it doesn't work.

363
00:22:14,830 --> 00:22:17,590
And furthermore, the softmax operation

364
00:22:17,590 --> 00:22:20,290
operates over the full batch.

365
00:22:20,290 --> 00:22:22,970
So it's not really very decomposable.

366
00:22:22,970 --> 00:22:25,090
Whereas if you think about normal language model

367
00:22:25,090 --> 00:22:30,250
training, a batch of examples, all the sequences parallelize

368
00:22:30,250 --> 00:22:37,810
and you just do aggregation at the end.

369
00:22:37,810 --> 00:22:42,930
So this point will be addressed by another paper from Google

370
00:22:42,930 --> 00:22:45,930
that came out called SigLIP.

371
00:22:45,930 --> 00:22:49,770
And so think about this as basically an improved version

372
00:22:49,770 --> 00:22:50,430
of CLIP.

373
00:22:50,430 --> 00:22:56,490
So it stands for Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-training.

374
00:22:56,490 --> 00:23:00,890
And here, the main difference is that CLIP does

375
00:23:00,890 --> 00:23:02,830
multi-class classification.

376
00:23:02,830 --> 00:23:05,250
It says, I have this aligned text and image.

377
00:23:05,250 --> 00:23:09,170
And I'm going to say this is positive against all

378
00:23:09,170 --> 00:23:11,890
the other alternative images or against all

379
00:23:11,890 --> 00:23:13,930
the alternative text.

380
00:23:13,930 --> 00:23:16,270
SigLIP, in some sense, is a lot simpler.

381
00:23:16,270 --> 00:23:21,070
It basically says for any given image text pair,

382
00:23:21,070 --> 00:23:23,190
are they aligned or not?

383
00:23:23,190 --> 00:23:27,030
So if I reference this diagram up here,

384
00:23:27,030 --> 00:23:28,870
it's basically binary classification

385
00:23:28,870 --> 00:23:31,030
where the diagonals are positive examples

386
00:23:31,030 --> 00:23:33,630
and the off-diagonals are negative examples.

387
00:23:33,630 --> 00:23:36,070
That's it.

388
00:23:36,070 --> 00:23:39,190
So here's the algorithm.

389
00:23:39,190 --> 00:23:40,930
You take the embeddings.

390
00:23:40,930 --> 00:23:41,970
You normalize.

391
00:23:41,970 --> 00:23:44,230
You take the dot product as usual.

392
00:23:44,230 --> 00:23:50,390
But now you're forming the labels which are minus 1

393
00:23:50,390 --> 00:23:53,950
on the off-diagonal, and diagonals you have one.

394
00:23:53,950 --> 00:24:00,030
And then you do log_sigmoid.

395
00:24:00,030 --> 00:24:02,050
So the objective is very simple.

396
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:19,000
Yeah, so the question is, does this

397
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:23,680
require any sophisticated sampling strategies?

398
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:26,120
At least in the initial paper, they

399
00:24:26,120 --> 00:24:31,280
were just operating on literally the same type of matrix.

400
00:24:31,280 --> 00:24:34,520
You could imagine, I think you're probably thinking in general

401
00:24:34,520 --> 00:24:37,840
for these contrastive methods.

402
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:40,280
The negative examples need to be—sometimes

403
00:24:40,280 --> 00:24:42,500
you need to balance the positives and the negatives,

404
00:24:42,500 --> 00:24:45,160
or the negatives need to be like tight negatives

405
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:46,700
so that you're not biased.

406
00:24:46,700 --> 00:24:49,180
But at least in the initial version,

407
00:24:49,180 --> 00:24:54,280
this was fairly straightforward.

408
00:24:54,280 --> 00:25:01,903
So to speak about the data, this was done at Google.

409
00:25:01,903 --> 00:25:02,820
This was the data set.

410
00:25:02,820 --> 00:25:06,280
They used another paper, which was another language image

411
00:25:06,280 --> 00:25:11,880
model around 2022, which got a WebLI data set with order

412
00:25:11,880 --> 00:25:14,660
billion image text pairs.

413
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:16,480
They also did some extra work.

414
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:18,400
For example, images that have text in them.

415
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:19,620
They did OCR.

416
00:25:19,620 --> 00:25:23,140
And that's another way to form text image pairs

417
00:25:23,140 --> 00:25:24,640
and did some filtering.

418
00:25:24,640 --> 00:25:26,920
This was multilingual.

419
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:33,780
The main point of this paper was that it was much more

420
00:25:33,780 --> 00:25:36,460
efficient to train than CLIP.

421
00:25:36,460 --> 00:25:42,540
So CLIP was trained 10 days on 256 TPUv3,

422
00:25:42,540 --> 00:25:46,260
and CLIP was five days on 32v4s.

423
00:25:46,260 --> 00:25:48,140
And you might think, oh, v4s actually

424
00:25:48,140 --> 00:25:49,700
should be faster than v3.

425
00:25:49,700 --> 00:25:53,140
In terms of flops per second, they're actually not.

426
00:25:53,140 --> 00:25:56,140
They're better because you can put more of them in a pod

427
00:25:56,140 --> 00:25:57,560
and the interconnect is better.

428
00:25:57,560 --> 00:26:04,500
But at this scale, it's really actually not faster.

429
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:09,660
Actually, 60% slower or something.

430
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:15,700
So the way that this is faster—and, of course,

431
00:26:15,700 --> 00:26:17,160
I think CLIP probably did not try

432
00:26:17,160 --> 00:26:21,180
to optimize the code for the maximum throughput.

433
00:26:21,180 --> 00:26:24,560
It was just let's get this thing to work.

434
00:26:24,560 --> 00:26:30,640
But the SigLIP paper showed that you can parallelize this

435
00:26:30,640 --> 00:26:31,420
as follows.

436
00:26:31,420 --> 00:26:36,500
So you have—basically, if you recall your systems lecture,

437
00:26:36,500 --> 00:26:41,400
so this is you can think about it as DDP if you like,

438
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:51,880
where each device stores a subset of the image text pairs.

439
00:26:51,880 --> 00:26:55,780
However, there's interactions between the examples,

440
00:26:55,780 --> 00:26:58,360
unlike in language model training, where everything just

441
00:26:58,360 --> 00:26:59,440
factors.

442
00:26:59,440 --> 00:27:02,560
So in the first pass, each device

443
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:09,840
just computes all the losses on whatever image text

444
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:11,410
pairs it has locally.

445
00:27:15,296 --> 00:27:19,150
And then you send the text.

446
00:27:19,150 --> 00:27:24,570
So basically, device 1 gets T5 through T8.

447
00:27:24,570 --> 00:27:27,270
And now it's able to compute the negatives here.

448
00:27:27,270 --> 00:27:30,110
And then it gets T9 through T12.

449
00:27:30,110 --> 00:27:35,290
So you rotate around until you cover all the off-diagonal block

450
00:27:35,290 --> 00:27:38,210
entries.

451
00:27:38,210 --> 00:27:42,850
Another thing that was nice about SigLIP

452
00:27:42,850 --> 00:27:48,250
is that you effectively decouple batch size from the loss.

453
00:27:48,250 --> 00:27:51,430
Whereas in CLIP, the loss is tied to the batch size.

454
00:27:51,430 --> 00:27:54,930
If you change the batch size, it's a different loss function.

455
00:27:54,930 --> 00:27:59,010
So they were able to experiment with much smaller batch sizes

456
00:27:59,010 --> 00:28:00,890
less than 16K.

457
00:28:00,890 --> 00:28:03,690
And in that regime, it's much better than CLIP

458
00:28:03,690 --> 00:28:05,210
because the loss function, if you

459
00:28:05,210 --> 00:28:10,490
have too many, too small a batch size, it just degrades.

460
00:28:10,490 --> 00:28:16,450
Whereas for smaller batch size for CLIP, you have more variance

461
00:28:16,450 --> 00:28:18,770
but it's the same in expectation.

462
00:28:18,770 --> 00:28:21,030
It's the same loss.

463
00:28:21,030 --> 00:28:24,730
They also say, well, you can go up to much larger batch sizes.

464
00:28:24,730 --> 00:28:26,390
But that doesn't really help.

465
00:28:26,390 --> 00:28:30,670
And we saw that critical batch sizes at some limit.

466
00:28:30,670 --> 00:28:34,370
And 32K was essentially their critical batch size.

467
00:28:37,070 --> 00:28:40,670
OK, so now we have CLIP and SigLIP.

468
00:28:40,670 --> 00:28:44,790
These are image encoders that take an image, in this version,

469
00:28:44,790 --> 00:28:49,510
just a fixed size image, 336 by 336, let's say, and maps it

470
00:28:49,510 --> 00:28:56,790
into a vector, OK, which contains some semantics.

471
00:28:56,790 --> 00:29:03,470
So now let's start building VLMs, Vision Language Models.

472
00:29:03,470 --> 00:29:08,000
And I'm going to talk about two families of models, LLaVA

473
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:09,920
and Qwen.

474
00:29:09,920 --> 00:29:13,830
The two are very similar in the broad template.

475
00:29:13,830 --> 00:29:15,580
There are some details that are different,

476
00:29:15,580 --> 00:29:18,800
which we'll go through, and the basic idea

477
00:29:18,800 --> 00:29:20,760
is that we're going to take these embeddings

478
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:23,640
and then just inject them into our language model.

479
00:29:23,640 --> 00:29:26,840
So this is going to be more of a flavor of mid-training

480
00:29:26,840 --> 00:29:30,420
or post-training, where we take an existing image encoder.

481
00:29:30,420 --> 00:29:33,040
We take an existing LLM, and then we're

482
00:29:33,040 --> 00:29:37,360
going to stitch it together rather than training something

483
00:29:37,360 --> 00:29:40,680
from scratch.

484
00:29:40,680 --> 00:29:46,680
So the LLaVA paper came out in 2023.

485
00:29:46,680 --> 00:29:51,080
And this got people excited because around this time,

486
00:29:51,080 --> 00:29:55,760
the closed models, like GPT, I think it was GPT-4,

487
00:29:55,760 --> 00:29:58,480
was able to do visual reasoning.

488
00:29:58,480 --> 00:30:02,240
And they were able to show that they could do

489
00:30:02,240 --> 00:30:03,660
some visual reasoning as well.

490
00:30:03,660 --> 00:30:07,320
It wasn't as good as GPT-4, of course,

491
00:30:07,320 --> 00:30:09,380
but I think it was an open model and people

492
00:30:09,380 --> 00:30:12,260
got to see what went under the hood.

493
00:30:12,260 --> 00:30:19,780
So for VLMs, there's essentially a few pieces here.

494
00:30:19,780 --> 00:30:21,340
There's the vision encoder.

495
00:30:21,340 --> 00:30:23,260
They use CLIP here.

496
00:30:23,260 --> 00:30:26,740
For the text decoder, they use a language model called Vicuna,

497
00:30:26,740 --> 00:30:30,500
which is the first Llama model that was fine-tuned

498
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:33,040
on some shared GPT conversations.

499
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:36,740
So these are conversations that people have with ChatGPT

500
00:30:36,740 --> 00:30:38,400
and share them on this website.

501
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:42,340
I don't think this is up anymore.

502
00:30:42,340 --> 00:30:50,080
So the data that they used was based on MS COCO.

503
00:30:50,080 --> 00:30:51,700
So this was an annotated data set

504
00:30:51,700 --> 00:30:55,300
where they had Mechanical Turk workers go and annotate

505
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:59,020
a bunch of images with bounding boxes and captions describing

506
00:30:59,020 --> 00:31:01,260
what was in the image.

507
00:31:01,260 --> 00:31:07,020
So they basically synthesized a data set for their model.

508
00:31:07,020 --> 00:31:11,600
So they prompted GPT-4 with the captions or the detected objects

509
00:31:11,600 --> 00:31:15,800
and GPT-4 to generate questions or conversations.

510
00:31:15,800 --> 00:31:20,800
For example, this is an image in the MS COCO data set.

511
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:24,660
This was an annotated caption done by a human.

512
00:31:24,660 --> 00:31:31,120
And also here is also annotated by a human.

513
00:31:31,120 --> 00:31:37,120
And then you ask GPT-4, generate me a conversation

514
00:31:37,120 --> 00:31:41,080
and you might get a question and an answer.

515
00:31:41,080 --> 00:31:44,780
You might ask GPT-4 generate me a detailed description.

516
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:47,660
It generates something, I guess, similar to the caption.

517
00:31:47,660 --> 00:31:50,980
And then you can ask GPT-4 to generate complex reasoning.

518
00:31:50,980 --> 00:31:54,532
And it does something like that.

519
00:31:54,532 --> 00:32:03,390
OK, so those are synthesized data, 158,000 examples.

520
00:32:03,390 --> 00:32:06,310
And then they trained a model on this.

521
00:32:06,310 --> 00:32:12,610
So the model-- so they use CLIP, in particular,

522
00:32:12,610 --> 00:32:18,090
the highest performing CLIP version, ViT-L/14.

523
00:32:18,090 --> 00:32:21,770
So 14 by 14 patches.

524
00:32:21,770 --> 00:32:25,850
And then they-- let's just look at this image.

525
00:32:25,850 --> 00:32:28,110
So they take images.

526
00:32:28,110 --> 00:32:30,410
They send it through this vision encoder CLIP.

527
00:32:30,410 --> 00:32:32,410
That gives you a vector.

528
00:32:32,410 --> 00:32:35,990
But this vector isn't really in the same space,

529
00:32:35,990 --> 00:32:38,770
so to speak, as the text.

530
00:32:38,770 --> 00:32:43,330
So then they multiply by a matrix W

531
00:32:43,330 --> 00:32:47,890
to get another vector, which is in the same space as the text

532
00:32:47,890 --> 00:32:49,010
embeddings.

533
00:32:49,010 --> 00:32:52,330
And for text, you basically just use your standard embeddings

534
00:32:52,330 --> 00:32:54,850
to get vectors.

535
00:32:54,850 --> 00:32:58,810
So what's basically happening is that the text gets encoded

536
00:32:58,810 --> 00:33:01,270
into these vectors and the image also gets

537
00:33:01,270 --> 00:33:04,030
encoded into these vectors.

538
00:33:04,030 --> 00:33:06,670
And then this whole sequence of vectors

539
00:33:06,670 --> 00:33:11,682
just goes through a standard Transformer and produces output.

540
00:33:11,682 --> 00:33:14,550
OK, so we're in some sense converting these images

541
00:33:14,550 --> 00:33:18,750
into textual tokens.

542
00:33:18,750 --> 00:33:21,770
So I can leverage the pre-trained language model.

543
00:33:24,510 --> 00:33:30,890
So to train this model, they do it in two stages.

544
00:33:30,890 --> 00:33:33,670
The first stage is that they freeze the vision

545
00:33:33,670 --> 00:33:39,070
encoder and the language model and only train this matrix W.

546
00:33:39,070 --> 00:33:42,150
And this is what they call the alignment phase, which

547
00:33:42,150 --> 00:33:46,830
is essentially making sure that the image gets

548
00:33:46,830 --> 00:33:50,090
mapped into the same space.

549
00:33:50,090 --> 00:33:54,890
Because if you just use a random W,

550
00:33:54,890 --> 00:33:58,520
these images are not going to be--

551
00:33:58,520 --> 00:34:00,320
I mean, certainly, they're not embeddings,

552
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:03,760
representing any natural language token.

553
00:34:03,760 --> 00:34:06,040
So the goal of training W is that they

554
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:09,840
look like natural language token embeddings.

555
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:12,840
And the second stage is that they now

556
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:15,000
still freeze a vision encoder and then train

557
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:16,219
W and the language model.

558
00:34:16,219 --> 00:34:22,520
So they're fine-tuning the language model on their data.

559
00:34:22,520 --> 00:34:26,040
So remember, their data is basically here's an image

560
00:34:26,040 --> 00:34:28,400
and here is a conversation or a description

561
00:34:28,400 --> 00:34:32,080
or a complex reasoning example.

562
00:34:32,080 --> 00:34:37,960
So this is all image plus text to text.

563
00:34:37,960 --> 00:34:43,440
OK, so this is one example from their paper.

564
00:34:43,440 --> 00:34:44,659
It's interesting.

565
00:34:44,659 --> 00:34:48,543
So you have an input image and a conversation.

566
00:34:48,543 --> 00:34:50,460
The user says what's unusual about this image.

567
00:34:50,460 --> 00:34:54,239
And their model is able to tell you that you don't usually

568
00:34:54,239 --> 00:34:57,380
iron on the back of a minivan.

569
00:34:57,380 --> 00:35:00,900
And they also make a point that their model is pretty good

570
00:35:00,900 --> 00:35:06,940
because even if your user prompt is not really

571
00:35:06,940 --> 00:35:12,180
prompting the unusualness, it still talks about it, whereas--

572
00:35:12,180 --> 00:35:14,520
GPT-4, obviously, is able to do this,

573
00:35:14,520 --> 00:35:20,650
but other models at the time were not able to.

574
00:35:20,650 --> 00:35:25,200
OK, any questions about LLaVA?

575
00:35:25,200 --> 00:35:29,100
OK, so that was 2023.

576
00:35:29,100 --> 00:35:35,800
And I'm going to skip forward to LLaVA OneVision, which is 2024.

577
00:35:35,800 --> 00:35:39,340
There is a sequence of papers after the LLaVA paper came out

578
00:35:39,340 --> 00:35:44,220
of LLaVA 1.5, LLaVA-Next.

579
00:35:44,220 --> 00:35:47,080
But I'll capture all of these innovations, I think,

580
00:35:47,080 --> 00:35:51,100
in what I'll describe next.

581
00:35:51,100 --> 00:35:55,000
The main thing that they did is it's essentially

582
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:56,960
the same recipe, but they are trying

583
00:35:56,960 --> 00:35:59,120
to be more ambitious in terms of the types

584
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:02,880
of multimodal applications they could do.

585
00:36:02,880 --> 00:36:06,680
So they were able to handle multiple images and videos.

586
00:36:06,680 --> 00:36:10,060
So here's the same diagram.

587
00:36:10,060 --> 00:36:15,400
Now they can take a single image or multiple images

588
00:36:15,400 --> 00:36:19,520
or a video, which is essentially a sequence of images

589
00:36:19,520 --> 00:36:23,920
corresponding to a sampling of the frames.

590
00:36:23,920 --> 00:36:26,060
And here are the vision encoder.

591
00:36:26,060 --> 00:36:28,920
They upgrade it to SigLIP.

592
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:31,980
The text decoder, they use Qwen-2 now,

593
00:36:31,980 --> 00:36:36,440
which was probably the best language model

594
00:36:36,440 --> 00:36:38,400
that was out at that time.

595
00:36:38,400 --> 00:36:42,160
And then for the projector or the adapter, which

596
00:36:42,160 --> 00:36:46,200
is the thing that takes the output of the vision encoder

597
00:36:46,200 --> 00:36:49,680
and turns it into the input of the language model

598
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:55,170
is a two-layer MLP instead of a linear projection.

599
00:36:55,170 --> 00:36:58,730
OK, so it's like you have a system

600
00:36:58,730 --> 00:37:01,090
and you're just upgrading the parts.

601
00:37:01,090 --> 00:37:05,370
But it's the same rough system.

602
00:37:05,370 --> 00:37:10,210
So let's talk a little bit about data processing.

603
00:37:10,210 --> 00:37:14,290
So one thing that they wanted to do is to do OCR.

604
00:37:14,290 --> 00:37:16,490
And the thing with OCR is that you

605
00:37:16,490 --> 00:37:20,130
need to preserve very fine-grained information.

606
00:37:20,130 --> 00:37:24,730
Otherwise, a J looks like an I, and that's not good.

607
00:37:24,730 --> 00:37:30,170
So remember that CLIP resizes and crops to 336 by 336.

608
00:37:30,170 --> 00:37:32,930
And clearly, if you have a document

609
00:37:32,930 --> 00:37:36,690
and you crop to 336 by 336, you can't read it.

610
00:37:36,690 --> 00:37:40,730
So their solution is this idea called

611
00:37:40,730 --> 00:37:46,210
AnyRes, which was actually introduced in the LLaVA 1.5 paper.

612
00:37:46,210 --> 00:37:49,870
And the idea is basically, I mean,

613
00:37:49,870 --> 00:37:51,150
it's fairly straightforward.

614
00:37:51,150 --> 00:37:56,830
You break up an image into multiple pieces.

615
00:37:56,830 --> 00:38:00,790
And the idea is that each piece is the resolution of whatever

616
00:38:00,790 --> 00:38:05,590
the vision encoder is going to take, 336 by 336, for example.

617
00:38:05,590 --> 00:38:09,890
So you're going to code all the pieces into a bunch of vectors.

618
00:38:09,890 --> 00:38:12,230
And then you concatenate the vectors.

619
00:38:12,230 --> 00:38:15,950
OK, so you're basically noticing that your vision encoder

620
00:38:15,950 --> 00:38:18,310
can't handle high resolution.

621
00:38:18,310 --> 00:38:20,710
So rather than downsampling, you're

622
00:38:20,710 --> 00:38:25,590
just going to crop and look at different parts of image.

623
00:38:25,590 --> 00:38:29,710
And now if you have really super high resolution images or videos

624
00:38:29,710 --> 00:38:31,330
then, you get too many tokens.

625
00:38:31,330 --> 00:38:35,230
So then they downsample.

626
00:38:35,230 --> 00:38:38,990
But the idea behind AnyRes is that it's adaptive.

627
00:38:38,990 --> 00:38:43,130
And this is nice because the transformer is already adaptive.

628
00:38:43,130 --> 00:38:46,230
Language can be-- your sentences can be any length

629
00:38:46,230 --> 00:38:48,750
and the transformer already handles that pretty well.

630
00:38:48,750 --> 00:38:51,660
So it turns out images can be any resolution.

631
00:38:51,660 --> 00:38:58,040
So that's picking back on the same dynamic ability.

632
00:38:58,040 --> 00:39:02,920
So here is what they actually do for images here.

633
00:39:02,920 --> 00:39:07,360
So let's say you have this image of a paper.

634
00:39:07,360 --> 00:39:11,560
They do downsample and have one path,

635
00:39:11,560 --> 00:39:15,480
which is basically a downsampled encoding of the entire paper.

636
00:39:15,480 --> 00:39:17,760
But then they break it up into these chunks,

637
00:39:17,760 --> 00:39:19,560
where each chunk is the resolution

638
00:39:19,560 --> 00:39:24,160
that the vision encoder expects, and then encodes each of these separately.

639
00:39:24,160 --> 00:39:26,860
And then you concatenate all these patches.

640
00:39:26,860 --> 00:39:28,760
And if you have too many, then you

641
00:39:28,760 --> 00:39:33,740
reduce the number of patches by interpolation.

642
00:39:36,800 --> 00:39:42,080
OK, so then they're able to handle images, multiple images,

643
00:39:42,080 --> 00:39:43,560
and video.

644
00:39:43,560 --> 00:39:46,800
So you think about, well, all of these

645
00:39:46,800 --> 00:39:49,940
are technically all reducible to images,

646
00:39:49,940 --> 00:39:52,300
but they put their thumb on the scale a little bit

647
00:39:52,300 --> 00:39:59,260
because they want to make sure all the modalities are roughly

648
00:39:59,260 --> 00:40:00,638
comparable.

649
00:40:00,638 --> 00:40:02,180
Because videos can be very long, they

650
00:40:02,180 --> 00:40:04,260
don't want their data set to be dominated

651
00:40:04,260 --> 00:40:07,180
by a bunch of repetitive frames.

652
00:40:07,180 --> 00:40:09,420
So here's what they do here.

653
00:40:09,420 --> 00:40:11,980
So for a single image, they basically

654
00:40:11,980 --> 00:40:14,900
have the full image downsampled and then

655
00:40:14,900 --> 00:40:20,260
a bunch of crops up to N crops there, up to 9.

656
00:40:20,260 --> 00:40:23,460
And if you are presented with multiple images,

657
00:40:23,460 --> 00:40:28,880
then they just use the base resolution.

658
00:40:28,880 --> 00:40:30,380
So if you have a single image, I get

659
00:40:30,380 --> 00:40:31,882
to look at it more carefully.

660
00:40:31,882 --> 00:40:33,340
If I have multiple images, I'm just

661
00:40:33,340 --> 00:40:37,780
going to look at it from afar.

662
00:40:37,780 --> 00:40:43,100
And for video, we're going to use even lower resolution

663
00:40:43,100 --> 00:40:46,920
or fewer tokens to represent each frame because the idea is

664
00:40:46,920 --> 00:40:54,160
that, well, videos can be-- they only use up to 32 frames,

665
00:40:54,160 --> 00:40:58,320
but you start running into context length

666
00:40:58,320 --> 00:41:01,280
problems for viewing.

667
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:04,760
And later, we'll see how a big part

668
00:41:04,760 --> 00:41:06,960
of being able to handle multimodal

669
00:41:06,960 --> 00:41:09,220
is to deal with longer context.

670
00:41:11,960 --> 00:41:22,400
OK, so the data here that they use is, I would say,

671
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:26,120
the state of philosophy is they want to curate high quality data

672
00:41:26,120 --> 00:41:27,120
and quantity.

673
00:41:27,120 --> 00:41:31,020
Another way to interpret this is that it's very targeted data.

674
00:41:31,020 --> 00:41:34,860
If you look at many of these, it's very task-based

675
00:41:34,860 --> 00:41:40,040
whether you're doing visual question answering or answering

676
00:41:40,040 --> 00:41:41,760
questions about tables.

677
00:41:41,760 --> 00:41:45,570
So this is definitely post-training territory,

678
00:41:45,570 --> 00:41:49,850
where you're trying to get your model to do these tasks,

679
00:41:49,850 --> 00:41:52,210
and therefore, you create a bunch of these tasks.

680
00:41:52,210 --> 00:41:57,970
And this work is also unabashedly, basically,

681
00:41:57,970 --> 00:42:01,890
distilling GPT-4 models so that they

682
00:42:01,890 --> 00:42:07,070
can get the best performance, which is, I guess, not ideal.

683
00:42:07,070 --> 00:42:14,530
But this is what you do if you don't have an annotation budget.

684
00:42:14,530 --> 00:42:20,390
And so they have single images, multiple images, and videos.

685
00:42:20,390 --> 00:42:24,870
So there's a lot of different pieces.

686
00:42:24,870 --> 00:42:26,630
Some of these are very specific.

687
00:42:26,630 --> 00:42:31,050
You give two images and you're trying to spot the difference.

688
00:42:31,050 --> 00:42:33,050
But one thing that--

689
00:42:33,050 --> 00:42:36,470
OK, I'll come back to this at this point later.

690
00:42:36,470 --> 00:42:42,550
So for training, it's roughly the same idea as before,

691
00:42:42,550 --> 00:42:48,190
where the first stage is that you're focused on alignment.

692
00:42:48,190 --> 00:42:54,830
So in particular, you only train the projector,

693
00:42:54,830 --> 00:42:58,510
which is the adapter.

694
00:42:58,510 --> 00:43:02,990
And then in the second stage and the third stages

695
00:43:02,990 --> 00:43:04,710
you train the full model.

696
00:43:04,710 --> 00:43:06,170
And before, we had two stages.

697
00:43:06,170 --> 00:43:09,950
Now, we have three stages.

698
00:43:09,950 --> 00:43:14,390
I'm not sure there's any particular principled reason

699
00:43:14,390 --> 00:43:16,230
for this, except for this second stage is

700
00:43:16,230 --> 00:43:19,810
trying to put high quality data but more focused on knowledge.

701
00:43:19,810 --> 00:43:24,110
And the final stage is focusing on examples that look

702
00:43:24,110 --> 00:43:25,890
like your downstream tasks.

703
00:43:30,230 --> 00:43:34,030
OK, so one thing that they found in this paper, which

704
00:43:34,030 --> 00:43:41,010
was interesting, is that you get transfer between modalities.

705
00:43:41,010 --> 00:43:44,570
So even though they have only single image data

706
00:43:44,570 --> 00:43:47,370
for diagrams and charts, but this actually

707
00:43:47,370 --> 00:43:49,070
generalizes to multiple images.

708
00:43:49,070 --> 00:43:54,130
So at training time, you never saw an example

709
00:43:54,130 --> 00:43:56,168
where you have a table and a chart

710
00:43:56,168 --> 00:43:57,710
and you're asking questions about it.

711
00:43:57,710 --> 00:44:04,130
But at test time, you have two images, one for the table

712
00:44:04,130 --> 00:44:05,270
and one for the chart.

713
00:44:05,270 --> 00:44:09,650
And you're able to have some of conversation about it.

714
00:44:09,650 --> 00:44:14,290
At training time, you only have OCR data on single image data,

715
00:44:14,290 --> 00:44:18,850
and you only have relational reasoning from multiple images.

716
00:44:18,850 --> 00:44:23,010
But you can generalize to these cases which

717
00:44:23,010 --> 00:44:25,430
are useful for powering GUI agents,

718
00:44:25,430 --> 00:44:28,150
where you show it a bunch of screenshots.

719
00:44:28,150 --> 00:44:32,410
This is multiple images and it can generate a description

720
00:44:32,410 --> 00:44:36,770
of the screenshots.

721
00:44:36,770 --> 00:44:39,220
Another example is that they have

722
00:44:39,220 --> 00:44:42,460
this idea of visual prompting, where

723
00:44:42,460 --> 00:44:45,640
you have this circle, which is a part of the image,

724
00:44:45,640 --> 00:44:48,180
but that's meant to highlight the part of the image

725
00:44:48,180 --> 00:44:51,860
that you're supposed to do something about.

726
00:44:51,860 --> 00:44:54,660
And this type of data only exists for single images.

727
00:44:54,660 --> 00:44:59,420
And now it can generalize to videos

728
00:44:59,420 --> 00:45:02,620
where the user says describe the player highlighted in the video.

729
00:45:02,620 --> 00:45:06,860
And this player is across multiple frames.

730
00:45:06,860 --> 00:45:11,940
So when you first look at this and say, oh, boy.

731
00:45:11,940 --> 00:45:14,120
You're basically targeting each of these tasks.

732
00:45:14,120 --> 00:45:16,600
It's supervised learning.

733
00:45:16,600 --> 00:45:19,020
But if you have enough tasks, these models

734
00:45:19,020 --> 00:45:26,560
seem to do some transfer, which is, I guess, reassuring.

735
00:45:29,300 --> 00:45:33,660
OK, so again, this is a fairly standard VLM

736
00:45:33,660 --> 00:45:37,680
template, vision encoder plus the projector into the language

737
00:45:37,680 --> 00:45:39,800
model.

738
00:45:39,800 --> 00:45:43,540
There's a lot of work that goes into the data curation.

739
00:45:43,540 --> 00:45:48,120
They lean pretty heavily into synthesizing task-specific data.

740
00:45:48,120 --> 00:45:49,960
The nice thing about the LLaVA series

741
00:45:49,960 --> 00:45:53,780
is that it's one of the few works that open sources.

742
00:45:53,780 --> 00:45:56,620
They're not just the model weights, but also the data.

743
00:45:56,620 --> 00:46:01,120
So you can really replicate and study this stuff.

744
00:46:01,120 --> 00:46:06,680
OK, now let's talk about the Qwen Models.

745
00:46:06,680 --> 00:46:11,860
So Qwen started also training in 2023 multimodal models.

746
00:46:11,860 --> 00:46:14,480
So the first version was Qwen-VL.

747
00:46:14,480 --> 00:46:17,360
And hopefully, I'll go through this

748
00:46:17,360 --> 00:46:19,780
quickly because you hopefully see the pattern.

749
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:23,560
The vision encoder, they use OpenCLIP.

750
00:46:23,560 --> 00:46:27,960
Remember, OpenCLIP was the open reproductions of the CLIP model.

751
00:46:27,960 --> 00:46:31,760
So it's basically a CLIP encoder.

752
00:46:31,760 --> 00:46:39,250
And for the adapter, they did one layer of cross-attention,

753
00:46:39,250 --> 00:46:42,970
incorporate 2D positional embeddings and maps to a fixed

754
00:46:42,970 --> 00:46:47,850
size of 256, which is a little bit--

755
00:46:51,210 --> 00:46:54,090
I guess this will be changed later because this is definitely

756
00:46:54,090 --> 00:46:56,730
not very dynamic, but neither is the vision encoder

757
00:46:56,730 --> 00:46:58,490
at this point.

758
00:46:58,490 --> 00:47:00,740
I guess this got cut off.

759
00:47:00,740 --> 00:47:03,810
But the special tokens, they have

760
00:47:03,810 --> 00:47:06,390
an image tag and a box tag for bounding box,

761
00:47:06,390 --> 00:47:10,450
and a ref tag for descriptions.

762
00:47:10,450 --> 00:47:14,410
This is what happens when your slides are in HTML.

763
00:47:14,410 --> 00:47:18,090
So for training, they have three stages, just

764
00:47:18,090 --> 00:47:19,830
like with the LLaVA models.

765
00:47:19,830 --> 00:47:23,090
In the first stage, the idea is to--

766
00:47:23,090 --> 00:47:26,930
I mean, they call it pre-training but you're not

767
00:47:26,930 --> 00:47:29,490
pre-training from scratch.

768
00:47:29,490 --> 00:47:33,350
This is generally large-scale, low quality data.

769
00:47:33,350 --> 00:47:38,150
They freeze the language model and train

770
00:47:38,150 --> 00:47:41,070
the vision encoder and adapter.

771
00:47:41,070 --> 00:47:45,390
So this is a bit different from the LLaVA models, in

772
00:47:45,390 --> 00:47:51,550
that, they train the vision encoder at this point.

773
00:47:51,550 --> 00:47:55,830
And you can see some of the data sets that they are training on,

774
00:47:55,830 --> 00:47:58,430
1.4 billion examples.

775
00:47:58,430 --> 00:48:02,770
Stage 2, they move to higher quality task-specific data.

776
00:48:02,770 --> 00:48:08,550
And you can see some of the usual suspects of VQA data sets,

777
00:48:08,550 --> 00:48:11,790
chart question answering, and so on.

778
00:48:11,790 --> 00:48:13,850
And then they train all the parameters here.

779
00:48:13,850 --> 00:48:19,790
And finally, stage 3 is instruction tuning data.

780
00:48:19,790 --> 00:48:25,710
You freeze the visual encoder and you train the adapter

781
00:48:25,710 --> 00:48:27,590
and the language model.

782
00:48:27,590 --> 00:48:29,670
Here are some examples of the type of things

783
00:48:29,670 --> 00:48:30,890
that they're able to do.

784
00:48:30,890 --> 00:48:33,110
So, of course, this is Qwen.

785
00:48:33,110 --> 00:48:37,570
So some of these examples are in Chinese.

786
00:48:37,570 --> 00:48:40,090
Their interest is also making a model

787
00:48:40,090 --> 00:48:44,350
that is supposed to be subsuming a language model.

788
00:48:44,350 --> 00:48:46,250
So a language model can do code.

789
00:48:46,250 --> 00:48:51,890
So we want the vision model to be good at code as well.

790
00:48:51,890 --> 00:48:56,630
And you can have this example, if you can see it, is an image.

791
00:48:56,630 --> 00:48:58,450
And can you find Spider-Man and the Hulk.

792
00:48:58,450 --> 00:49:01,770
And it can output bounding boxes as well.

793
00:49:01,770 --> 00:49:03,270
It doesn't actually output an image.

794
00:49:03,270 --> 00:49:07,070
It just outputs a bounding box.

795
00:49:10,090 --> 00:49:11,730
And then you can do OCR.

796
00:49:11,730 --> 00:49:16,730
You can compare-- so usual stuff.

797
00:49:16,730 --> 00:49:22,150
OK, so then Qwen-2 was, again, an upgrade.

798
00:49:22,150 --> 00:49:25,650
There's some new ideas here, which I'll go through.

799
00:49:25,650 --> 00:49:29,010
So use a larger vision encoder.

800
00:49:29,010 --> 00:49:32,900
They also started doing dynamic resolution.

801
00:49:32,900 --> 00:49:34,540
I think this was the main thing.

802
00:49:34,540 --> 00:49:37,420
We remember when we went from LLaVA to LLaVA OneVision,

803
00:49:37,420 --> 00:49:39,980
they realized, oh, we need to handle

804
00:49:39,980 --> 00:49:42,440
images of different sizes.

805
00:49:42,440 --> 00:49:44,620
And if you try to do video, it's clear

806
00:49:44,620 --> 00:49:47,700
that you need some dynamic resolution.

807
00:49:47,700 --> 00:49:54,940
So the model is basically-- so let's say you have this picture

808
00:49:54,940 --> 00:50:00,660
here, that might be mapped to 11,000 tokens.

809
00:50:00,660 --> 00:50:06,140
This tiny picture of an equation might be only mapped to 8 tokens

810
00:50:06,140 --> 00:50:09,380
and so on and so forth.

811
00:50:09,380 --> 00:50:13,220
So how do they do this?

812
00:50:13,220 --> 00:50:18,620
Well, basically each-- it's the same idea AnyRes idea

813
00:50:18,620 --> 00:50:26,980
from LLaVA, where each 224 by 224 patch is encoded with a ViT.

814
00:50:26,980 --> 00:50:30,480
And remember the ViT that they started

815
00:50:30,480 --> 00:50:32,920
with is the OpenCLIP bit.

816
00:50:32,920 --> 00:50:36,880
But this gets fine-tuned.

817
00:50:36,880 --> 00:50:40,060
And then trying to reduce the context size,

818
00:50:40,060 --> 00:50:43,200
they compress every 2 by 2 into 1

819
00:50:43,200 --> 00:50:50,280
so that they ultimately each every patch generates 66 tokens.

820
00:50:50,280 --> 00:50:54,640
For eight videos, they sample 2 frames a second but max out

821
00:50:54,640 --> 00:50:59,600
at 16,000 tokens.

822
00:50:59,600 --> 00:51:01,800
OK, so one thing coming back to this question

823
00:51:01,800 --> 00:51:05,840
about positional embeddings, so they did

824
00:51:05,840 --> 00:51:09,360
do something different here with Qwen-2.

825
00:51:09,360 --> 00:51:14,320
So they used this idea called multimodal rotary, multimodal

826
00:51:14,320 --> 00:51:15,200
RoPE.

827
00:51:15,200 --> 00:51:17,640
So remember, we saw RoPE before.

828
00:51:17,640 --> 00:51:22,400
And the idea behind RoPE is that you have embedding such

829
00:51:22,400 --> 00:51:26,930
that the inner product between the vectors

830
00:51:26,930 --> 00:51:29,490
depends only on the distance.

831
00:51:29,490 --> 00:51:34,790
So the distance is defined in 1D by just the number of tokens.

832
00:51:34,790 --> 00:51:36,450
You're away from it.

833
00:51:36,450 --> 00:51:39,130
So now, the multidimensional version

834
00:51:39,130 --> 00:51:41,030
is essentially the same thing.

835
00:51:41,030 --> 00:51:44,490
Except for now you have 3D.

836
00:51:44,490 --> 00:51:45,630
So you have height.

837
00:51:45,630 --> 00:51:47,690
You have width and you have time.

838
00:51:47,690 --> 00:51:53,610
So each position, each patch now is a triple

839
00:51:53,610 --> 00:51:56,050
defined by the coordinates.

840
00:51:56,050 --> 00:52:01,850
And then to compute the M-RoPE, basically, for every dimension,

841
00:52:01,850 --> 00:52:04,610
you compute the RoPE and then you concatenate.

842
00:52:04,610 --> 00:52:09,290
So it's a fairly straightforward idea.

843
00:52:09,290 --> 00:52:11,610
Although, later, with Qwen-3, we'll

844
00:52:11,610 --> 00:52:17,330
see how this is actually a bit suboptimal.

845
00:52:17,330 --> 00:52:22,170
So they initialize the language model with--

846
00:52:22,170 --> 00:52:23,790
this is the Qwen-VL model.

847
00:52:23,790 --> 00:52:26,510
So, of course, they're initializing with Qwen.

848
00:52:26,510 --> 00:52:30,190
And then they have three stages of training,

849
00:52:30,190 --> 00:52:34,670
which is very similar to what we did before.

850
00:52:34,670 --> 00:52:37,150
And then they were able to show a bunch

851
00:52:37,150 --> 00:52:41,990
of different capabilities, some video understanding.

852
00:52:41,990 --> 00:52:46,790
It can do math and code, function calling,

853
00:52:46,790 --> 00:52:50,590
and so on and so forth.

854
00:52:50,590 --> 00:52:53,310
OK, so quickly, I'll talk about Qwen-3.

855
00:52:56,110 --> 00:52:59,950
So again, there's a few changes here.

856
00:52:59,950 --> 00:53:03,010
I wouldn't say these are structural big changes,

857
00:53:03,010 --> 00:53:05,830
but they're probably changes that do impact

858
00:53:05,830 --> 00:53:07,210
the quality of the model.

859
00:53:07,210 --> 00:53:11,470
So this is from last year Qwen3-VL report.

860
00:53:11,470 --> 00:53:16,710
And so it's roughly the same diagram here.

861
00:53:16,710 --> 00:53:20,770
I'll point out maybe--I'll come back to this diagram.

862
00:53:20,770 --> 00:53:24,190
I'll go through some of the changes.

863
00:53:24,190 --> 00:53:26,830
So one is that now they're using Qwen-3 models.

864
00:53:26,830 --> 00:53:29,270
They have a series of dense and MoE models.

865
00:53:29,270 --> 00:53:32,350
And the Qwen-3 models are really, really good,

866
00:53:32,350 --> 00:53:35,890
and it really helps the quality of the final model.

867
00:53:35,890 --> 00:53:39,430
One thing that they're invested in is long context understanding.

868
00:53:39,430 --> 00:53:42,770
So their context length can go up to 256K,

869
00:53:42,770 --> 00:53:48,290
which is really important if you're trying to do long video.

870
00:53:48,290 --> 00:53:50,730
Let's talk about the vision encoder.

871
00:53:50,730 --> 00:53:53,610
So they use SigLIP-2, which is basically

872
00:53:53,610 --> 00:53:55,350
an improved version of SigLIP.

873
00:53:55,350 --> 00:53:56,970
It's actually identical architecture,

874
00:53:56,970 --> 00:54:00,650
is designed to be backward compatible.

875
00:54:00,650 --> 00:54:04,490
And then they improved their RoPE.

876
00:54:04,490 --> 00:54:10,250
And the way to explain this is, before, you

877
00:54:10,250 --> 00:54:13,490
Had the three dimensions: time, width, and height.

878
00:54:13,490 --> 00:54:20,330
And basically, when to basically allocate

879
00:54:20,330 --> 00:54:24,460
the first block of dimensions to the time,

880
00:54:24,460 --> 00:54:28,060
then the next block to width and the next to height.

881
00:54:28,060 --> 00:54:31,320
And the problem, if you remember RoPE

882
00:54:31,320 --> 00:54:34,740
is that each component represents

883
00:54:34,740 --> 00:54:36,980
a different frequency.

884
00:54:36,980 --> 00:54:39,860
So this would mean that maybe all the temporal dimensions

885
00:54:39,860 --> 00:54:42,220
are low frequency and all the high dimensions

886
00:54:42,220 --> 00:54:43,940
are high frequency.

887
00:54:43,940 --> 00:54:48,920
So they basically interleave them when they realize that.

888
00:54:48,920 --> 00:54:55,660
And now all the axes are exposed

889
00:54:55,660 --> 00:54:59,300
to both low and high frequency.

890
00:54:59,300 --> 00:55:04,740
Another thing they did was explicit video timestamps.

891
00:55:04,740 --> 00:55:08,840
So if you look closely at this--

892
00:55:12,020 --> 00:55:12,900
wait.

893
00:55:12,900 --> 00:55:14,900
OK, this is Qwen-3.

894
00:55:14,900 --> 00:55:16,300
Oops.

895
00:55:16,300 --> 00:55:19,580
So if you look--this is probably a little bit too small.

896
00:55:19,580 --> 00:55:25,000
But before, the timestamp was implicit

897
00:55:25,000 --> 00:55:28,280
in the positional encodings.

898
00:55:28,280 --> 00:55:30,600
Each frame in the video intrinsically

899
00:55:30,600 --> 00:55:33,920
gets a different-- there's a notion of time

900
00:55:33,920 --> 00:55:37,120
just because it's in a different position.

901
00:55:37,120 --> 00:55:39,620
But here, they made the time explicit.

902
00:55:39,620 --> 00:55:42,040
So these are actually tokens: zero seconds

903
00:55:42,040 --> 00:55:45,640
is a token--now you have a token that

904
00:55:45,640 --> 00:55:47,880
represents the time.

905
00:55:47,880 --> 00:55:51,812
And they found this to be helpful, presumably because it's

906
00:55:51,812 --> 00:55:53,520
something that you can directly refer to,

907
00:55:53,520 --> 00:55:56,300
like what happened after two seconds.

908
00:55:59,080 --> 00:56:05,520
So that is one of the main changes.

909
00:56:05,520 --> 00:56:10,440
They also did this square root normalized per token loss.

910
00:56:10,440 --> 00:56:14,320
So the idea is that some examples that

911
00:56:14,320 --> 00:56:18,260
involve video are very long and some examples that involve

912
00:56:18,260 --> 00:56:20,460
a single image are very short.

913
00:56:20,460 --> 00:56:24,780
And the normal thing is that every token is treated the same.

914
00:56:24,780 --> 00:56:26,940
And this would mean that the video examples

915
00:56:26,940 --> 00:56:29,700
are going to dominate.

916
00:56:29,700 --> 00:56:33,000
So they basically-- the details aren't too clear.

917
00:56:33,000 --> 00:56:35,860
But I believe they normalized each example

918
00:56:35,860 --> 00:56:41,940
by the square root of the length to basically downweight

919
00:56:41,940 --> 00:56:46,500
the impact of really long examples.

920
00:56:46,500 --> 00:56:49,600
The final thing that is worth noting, which is interesting,

921
00:56:49,600 --> 00:56:51,900
is that they did something interesting with adapter.

922
00:56:51,900 --> 00:56:53,820
So remember we started the adapter is

923
00:56:53,820 --> 00:56:56,260
the thing that connects the vision encoder to the language

924
00:56:56,260 --> 00:56:56,980
model.

925
00:56:56,980 --> 00:56:58,407
And LLaVA was the simplest.

926
00:56:58,407 --> 00:56:59,740
It was just a linear projection.

927
00:56:59,740 --> 00:57:01,180
And then we got an MLP.

928
00:57:01,180 --> 00:57:03,100
And then we got some cross-attention.

929
00:57:03,100 --> 00:57:08,060
And DeepStack, which is actually a paper from the DeepSeek team,

930
00:57:08,060 --> 00:57:14,020
but when used here is more sophisticated.

931
00:57:14,020 --> 00:57:18,030
They noticed that, well, the vision encoder already computes

932
00:57:18,030 --> 00:57:23,350
a stack of vision embeddings.

933
00:57:23,350 --> 00:57:26,870
And we're basically going to basically add

934
00:57:26,870 --> 00:57:34,790
these directly into the residual stream of the language model.

935
00:57:34,790 --> 00:57:42,630
OK, so this is a bit more of a deep fusion of the vision

936
00:57:42,630 --> 00:57:47,750
encoder into the language model as opposed to the vision encoder

937
00:57:47,750 --> 00:57:51,410
is a black box that just outputs a sequence of vectors.

938
00:57:54,430 --> 00:58:00,390
OK, so training is--so they have two phases.

939
00:58:00,390 --> 00:58:02,718
Pre-training has actually four stages.

940
00:58:02,718 --> 00:58:04,010
Post-training has three stages.

941
00:58:04,010 --> 00:58:06,510
So you can see that this pipelines are

942
00:58:06,510 --> 00:58:10,430
getting quite complicated now.

943
00:58:10,430 --> 00:58:11,890
So they first train the adapter.

944
00:58:11,890 --> 00:58:14,850
This is the same as all of these models.

945
00:58:14,850 --> 00:58:18,170
And then they have three stages where they progressively train

946
00:58:18,170 --> 00:58:25,290
on longer and longer sequences, from 8K, to 32K, to 256K.

947
00:58:25,290 --> 00:58:32,090
Most of the tokens are in the stages 2 and 3.

948
00:58:32,090 --> 00:58:34,530
And then for post-training, they do

949
00:58:34,530 --> 00:58:39,430
SFT on long chain of thought data, knowledge distillation,

950
00:58:39,430 --> 00:58:43,730
and then some reinforcement learning.

951
00:58:43,730 --> 00:58:46,070
At this point, it's really a systems paper.

952
00:58:46,070 --> 00:58:51,190
There's a bunch of--I highlighted the core new ideas,

953
00:58:51,190 --> 00:58:54,610
but there's a lot of details that are different.

954
00:58:54,610 --> 00:58:56,230
And if you look at the final results,

955
00:58:56,230 --> 00:58:59,150
this is a pretty good model.

956
00:58:59,150 --> 00:59:01,690
I mean, if you look at these benchmarks as compared

957
00:59:01,690 --> 00:59:09,136
to the closed models Gemini, GPT-5, and Opus 4.1,

958
00:59:09,136 --> 00:59:11,090
these numbers are--

959
00:59:11,090 --> 00:59:15,140
the bold means that it's the best number in that row.

960
00:59:15,140 --> 00:59:18,320
So the Qwen models are actually quite strong.

961
00:59:21,340 --> 00:59:25,450
OK, maybe the-- yeah, a question.

962
00:59:54,900 --> 00:59:59,860
Yeah, so the question is what about video generation

963
00:59:59,860 --> 01:00:02,480
and how do you know whether there should be a video or text?

964
01:00:02,480 --> 01:00:07,260
So first of all, these models don't generate videos or images.

965
01:00:07,260 --> 01:00:09,660
All the multimodal stuff is on the input side.

966
01:00:09,660 --> 01:00:11,900
You're always generating text.

967
01:00:16,200 --> 01:00:21,760
And then you're always in most of these stages, except for RL,

968
01:00:21,760 --> 01:00:24,040
you're supervising every token.

969
01:00:24,040 --> 01:00:31,040
So there's no LLM as a judge or quality on how well

970
01:00:31,040 --> 01:00:32,220
the description is working.

971
01:00:32,220 --> 01:00:34,800
It's just basically whatever is in your data set.

972
01:00:34,800 --> 01:00:39,520
Now, with RL, you can play around with different rewards.

973
01:00:39,520 --> 01:00:41,040
Yeah.

974
01:00:41,040 --> 01:00:45,280
I'll ask is the training of multimodal models

975
01:00:45,280 --> 01:00:51,780
harder than training a pure LLM from a system perspective?

976
01:01:16,860 --> 01:01:22,500
Yeah, so the two questions are trained multimodal models harder

977
01:01:22,500 --> 01:01:24,020
on the system side?

978
01:01:24,020 --> 01:01:27,820
And, I mean, certainly, it's not easier.

979
01:01:27,820 --> 01:01:32,120
I think you pointed out that the data sets are larger.

980
01:01:32,120 --> 01:01:36,000
Video data is just-- even loading it can be a bottleneck.

981
01:01:36,000 --> 01:01:38,580
Whereas generally, we have not really

982
01:01:38,580 --> 01:01:41,700
focused on data loading for talking about language models

983
01:01:41,700 --> 01:01:44,600
because it's very, very cheap.

984
01:01:44,600 --> 01:01:47,360
So that needs to be taken into account.

985
01:01:47,360 --> 01:01:49,740
And all the same principles of making sure

986
01:01:49,740 --> 01:01:51,420
that your data loading is happening

987
01:01:51,420 --> 01:01:56,300
async with actual computation needs to be taken into account.

988
01:01:56,300 --> 01:01:59,300
And the second part was--

989
01:01:59,300 --> 01:02:01,200
sorry, what was the second question?

990
01:02:01,200 --> 01:02:03,560
Oh, yeah.

991
01:02:03,560 --> 01:02:07,950
So the videos and images can have more-- especially

992
01:02:07,950 --> 01:02:09,590
video have more tokens.

993
01:02:09,590 --> 01:02:14,270
So that's one reason we saw this normalization

994
01:02:14,270 --> 01:02:19,192
to make sure that we're not giving more weight to video.

995
01:02:19,192 --> 01:02:20,650
I mean, you can always down weight.

996
01:02:20,650 --> 01:02:22,775
So if you have more tokens, if you don't like that,

997
01:02:22,775 --> 01:02:24,110
you can down weight.

998
01:02:24,110 --> 01:02:29,110
We talked in the last or two weeks ago about data mixtures.

999
01:02:29,110 --> 01:02:31,390
So you can always weight things according

1000
01:02:31,390 --> 01:02:35,750
to whatever makes sense.

1001
01:02:35,750 --> 01:02:38,870
But there's also a lot of text tokens out there.

1002
01:02:38,870 --> 01:02:44,090
So I think, in general, if you look at, yeah,

1003
01:02:44,090 --> 01:02:48,550
the models are trained on tens of trillions of tokens.

1004
01:02:48,550 --> 01:02:50,310
I think there's a-- yeah, I wouldn't

1005
01:02:50,310 --> 01:02:52,990
say that the number of multimodal tokens

1006
01:02:52,990 --> 01:02:56,470
vastly outnumbers text tokens.

1007
01:02:56,470 --> 01:03:00,370
Yeah, oh, I think you were first.

1008
01:03:23,010 --> 01:03:26,170
So the question is, when you do this alignment,

1009
01:03:26,170 --> 01:03:29,530
how does it know what the language-- is the model language

1010
01:03:29,530 --> 01:03:31,350
model pre-trained?

1011
01:03:31,350 --> 01:03:34,930
And it definitely has to be pre-trained because otherwise it

1012
01:03:34,930 --> 01:03:36,710
doesn't make sense to align it.

1013
01:03:36,710 --> 01:03:38,510
So the language model is frozen.

1014
01:03:38,510 --> 01:03:40,370
And you're just training the adapter

1015
01:03:40,370 --> 01:03:47,330
to connect the given vision encoder with a given language

1016
01:03:47,330 --> 01:03:48,210
model.

1017
01:03:48,210 --> 01:03:50,350
And the way you train it is, well,

1018
01:03:50,350 --> 01:03:54,810
you pick a token budget, 67 billion tokens

1019
01:03:54,810 --> 01:03:56,150
and you just train.

1020
01:03:56,150 --> 01:04:00,090
There's not an adaptive threshold here.

1021
01:04:00,090 --> 01:04:00,610
Yeah.

1022
01:04:32,340 --> 01:04:36,220
Yeah, so the question is, is the number

1023
01:04:36,220 --> 01:04:39,220
of parameters in the vision encoder much smaller?

1024
01:04:39,220 --> 01:04:41,820
And the answer is yes, in general.

1025
01:04:41,820 --> 01:04:45,520
I'm trying to find--where is the other place I--

1026
01:04:48,820 --> 01:04:49,640
not here.

1027
01:04:49,640 --> 01:04:54,580
OK, the reason is that the vision encoder

1028
01:04:54,580 --> 01:04:57,960
is in some sense doing a very local operation.

1029
01:04:57,960 --> 01:04:58,960
It's looking at a patch.

1030
01:04:58,960 --> 01:05:00,460
A patch is very small.

1031
01:05:00,460 --> 01:05:03,500
And it's just trying to understand the patches.

1032
01:05:03,500 --> 01:05:05,480
There's not much knowledge.

1033
01:05:05,480 --> 01:05:07,460
So the patch, we're not reasoning.

1034
01:05:07,460 --> 01:05:10,200
So most of the capabilities of the model

1035
01:05:10,200 --> 01:05:14,560
are still in the language model.

1036
01:05:14,560 --> 01:05:19,240
I'm trying to find the--OK, where?

1037
01:05:19,240 --> 01:05:21,440
Maybe it was up here.

1038
01:05:21,440 --> 01:05:23,680
OK, I can't-- oh, yeah.

1039
01:05:23,680 --> 01:05:28,160
So if you look at the LLaVA model, so the full--

1040
01:05:28,160 --> 01:05:30,720
so 72 billion parameter language model.

1041
01:05:35,640 --> 01:05:36,160
Sorry.

1042
01:05:36,160 --> 01:05:46,220
What-- OK, I don't know why this number is so-- oh, sorry.

1043
01:05:46,220 --> 01:05:47,700
This is 72 million, OK.

1044
01:05:47,700 --> 01:05:49,980
So this is much smaller.

1045
01:05:49,980 --> 01:05:51,380
The projector is much smaller.

1046
01:05:51,380 --> 01:05:58,680
And the ViT is generally less than a billion parameters.

1047
01:05:58,680 --> 01:06:05,000
Yeah, I guess mostly because it's fairly a local operation.

1048
01:06:05,000 --> 01:06:07,380
I guess that's the easy way to say it.

1049
01:06:07,380 --> 01:06:09,400
OK, I'm going to think some other questions.

1050
01:06:09,400 --> 01:06:11,360
I'm going to try to move on now.

1051
01:06:13,870 --> 01:06:18,860
So Qwen-3 is the last vision language model.

1052
01:06:18,860 --> 01:06:24,340
I'll talk about this SOTA, Save Our Performance.

1053
01:06:24,340 --> 01:06:28,660
There's a lot of data work that happens.

1054
01:06:28,660 --> 01:06:31,060
Not too many details about the data

1055
01:06:31,060 --> 01:06:35,580
and the data mix in these later Qwen models.

1056
01:06:35,580 --> 01:06:41,580
So you'll have to look at the LLaVA paper or the--

1057
01:06:41,580 --> 01:06:43,620
AI2 has a [INAUDIBLE] paper, which

1058
01:06:43,620 --> 01:06:45,980
I didn't get a chance to talk about that

1059
01:06:45,980 --> 01:06:49,420
has more details here.

1060
01:06:49,420 --> 01:06:56,660
And from Qwen to Qwen-2, Qwen-3, many of these changes were

1061
01:06:56,660 --> 01:07:01,910
mostly--there's some changes there.

1062
01:07:01,910 --> 01:07:06,110
But the overall framework remains the same.

1063
01:07:06,110 --> 01:07:09,690
And mostly, it's scaling up, curating more data sets,

1064
01:07:09,690 --> 01:07:13,630
noticing that your context isn't-- you have to handle long

1065
01:07:13,630 --> 01:07:18,990
context and maybe sharpening your image processing and so on.

1066
01:07:18,990 --> 01:07:26,550
OK, so I'm going to now jump to something quite different.

1067
01:07:26,550 --> 01:07:32,110
So there's this paper called Chameleon from Meta in 2024.

1068
01:07:32,110 --> 01:07:37,370
So, so far, VLMs encode images into these vectors

1069
01:07:37,370 --> 01:07:40,550
and then they inject them into a language model.

1070
01:07:40,550 --> 01:07:42,430
And because it's a language model,

1071
01:07:42,430 --> 01:07:44,890
you can only generate text.

1072
01:07:44,890 --> 01:07:49,550
You can't generate images.

1073
01:07:49,550 --> 01:07:53,850
So there's many ways you can go about fixing this.

1074
01:07:53,850 --> 01:07:55,530
You can just take a VLM.

1075
01:07:55,530 --> 01:07:57,570
You can also attach a diffusion head.

1076
01:07:57,570 --> 01:08:00,170
And now you can generate images or—

1077
01:08:00,170 --> 01:08:04,170
but I want to talk about this idea here,

1078
01:08:04,170 --> 01:08:07,570
which is Chameleon says, what if we mapped everything

1079
01:08:07,570 --> 01:08:10,450
into discrete tokens?

1080
01:08:10,450 --> 01:08:14,210
And in some ways aesthetically this is—

1081
01:08:14,210 --> 01:08:16,430
well, maybe this reflects that I'm a language person.

1082
01:08:16,430 --> 01:08:22,170
This is appealing because now you can analyze and generate

1083
01:08:22,170 --> 01:08:23,569
images in the same way.

1084
01:08:23,569 --> 01:08:25,370
Everything is a discrete token.

1085
01:08:25,370 --> 01:08:30,410
If you want to do what—

1086
01:08:30,410 --> 01:08:33,550
let's say you want to generate images.

1087
01:08:33,550 --> 01:08:37,229
You can say, here's a text prompt.

1088
01:08:37,229 --> 01:08:40,649
And then you can just— these are more just

1089
01:08:40,649 --> 01:08:42,330
tokens that you can generate.

1090
01:08:42,330 --> 01:08:49,330
Or you can flip the language and the text around as well.

1091
01:08:49,330 --> 01:08:52,770
They have these examples from the Chameleon paper

1092
01:08:52,770 --> 01:08:55,319
you can prompt in say I'm bored.

1093
01:08:55,319 --> 01:08:56,479
Can you show me some birds?

1094
01:08:56,479 --> 01:08:58,220
And then it would be text, and then it

1095
01:08:58,220 --> 01:09:01,439
would be images, and then some more text and some images.

1096
01:09:01,439 --> 01:09:03,979
So it's interleaved.

1097
01:09:03,979 --> 01:09:06,899
So in some sense, the vision of an omni model

1098
01:09:06,899 --> 01:09:10,800
is that text and images truly live in the same space.

1099
01:09:10,800 --> 01:09:15,000
And this is accomplished by making everything look like text.

1100
01:09:15,000 --> 01:09:16,620
That's one way to do it.

1101
01:09:16,620 --> 01:09:20,120
OK, so I'll talk a little bit about how this is done.

1102
01:09:20,120 --> 01:09:28,380
So the thing you need to do now is to map images

1103
01:09:28,380 --> 01:09:30,740
into discrete tokens.

1104
01:09:30,740 --> 01:09:38,460
So there's this idea rather old from Oord 2017,

1105
01:09:38,460 --> 01:09:41,740
which is called vector quantized variational autoencoder.

1106
01:09:41,740 --> 01:09:47,180
And the basic idea here is to map an image

1107
01:09:47,180 --> 01:09:51,060
into a discrete code.

1108
01:09:51,060 --> 01:09:54,080
And what you learn is a mapping that maps this

1109
01:09:54,080 --> 01:09:57,080
into a continuous vector.

1110
01:09:57,080 --> 01:10:00,840
And this gets rounded to the nearest code.

1111
01:10:00,840 --> 01:10:06,060
So you have a codebook which has, let's say, 8,000 codes.

1112
01:10:06,060 --> 01:10:11,040
So these are prototypical vectors that correspond to what

1113
01:10:11,040 --> 01:10:12,320
patches.

1114
01:10:12,320 --> 01:10:16,600
And then so you pick one of these that's the closest.

1115
01:10:16,600 --> 01:10:19,120
And that would be your representation.

1116
01:10:19,120 --> 01:10:20,163
So that's your encoder.

1117
01:10:20,163 --> 01:10:21,580
It goes through a continuous phase

1118
01:10:21,580 --> 01:10:25,080
and then you round it to the nearest code.

1119
01:10:25,080 --> 01:10:27,640
And now the decoder is you take that code

1120
01:10:27,640 --> 01:10:29,800
and you try to reconstruct the image.

1121
01:10:29,800 --> 01:10:36,480
And then you train these VQ-VAEs by basically minimizing

1122
01:10:36,480 --> 01:10:38,680
the reconstruction loss.

1123
01:10:38,680 --> 01:10:40,240
There are some other terms that you

1124
01:10:40,240 --> 01:10:43,160
add because this is not differentiable, which

1125
01:10:43,160 --> 01:10:46,640
I won't have time to get into.

1126
01:10:46,640 --> 01:10:52,500
OK, but, at the end of the day, you have 512 by 512 images that

1127
01:10:52,500 --> 01:10:59,980
are converted into 1,024 tokens, and each token comes from

1128
01:10:59,980 --> 01:11:03,140
a vocabulary of 8,000.

1129
01:11:03,140 --> 01:11:06,440
OK, so now you have basically text.

1130
01:11:06,440 --> 01:11:12,020
You have your images, which are basically these codes.

1131
01:11:12,020 --> 01:11:16,820
They train a new tokenizer because now you

1132
01:11:16,820 --> 01:11:19,460
have different-- your data looks different than if it were

1133
01:11:19,460 --> 01:11:24,060
just normal natural language.

1134
01:11:24,060 --> 01:11:29,520
The training is now actually very straightforward.

1135
01:11:29,520 --> 01:11:31,880
This is just normal language model training.

1136
01:11:31,880 --> 01:11:32,920
There's no adapter.

1137
01:11:32,920 --> 01:11:36,860
There's no turn the vision encoder.

1138
01:11:36,860 --> 01:11:39,940
There's just a language model now.

1139
01:11:39,940 --> 01:11:44,020
So this is the part that's appealing.

1140
01:11:44,020 --> 01:11:46,560
So there's still two stages.

1141
01:11:46,560 --> 01:11:48,420
But even for language model training,

1142
01:11:48,420 --> 01:11:51,030
there's usually two or more stages,

1143
01:11:51,030 --> 01:11:53,690
where the first stage is the bulk of training.

1144
01:11:53,690 --> 01:11:55,710
It's largely unsupervised.

1145
01:11:55,710 --> 01:11:59,950
They have both text and text and image.

1146
01:11:59,950 --> 01:12:04,950
And then stage 2, they mix in high quality data.

1147
01:12:04,950 --> 01:12:09,430
OK, so this is really great and elegant.

1148
01:12:09,430 --> 01:12:11,750
There's a few problems with this.

1149
01:12:11,750 --> 01:12:16,510
One is that they found that the training was not stable.

1150
01:12:16,510 --> 01:12:20,110
And the reason is that text and images,

1151
01:12:20,110 --> 01:12:22,550
despite occupying the same space,

1152
01:12:22,550 --> 01:12:25,230
just behave very differently.

1153
01:12:25,230 --> 01:12:30,530
So just calling things discrete tokens

1154
01:12:30,530 --> 01:12:33,630
isn't hiding the fact that there's an image living there.

1155
01:12:33,630 --> 01:12:36,590
And in particular, text tokens, if you

1156
01:12:36,590 --> 01:12:38,410
think about predicting the next word,

1157
01:12:38,410 --> 01:12:40,890
this has relatively lower entropy.

1158
01:12:40,890 --> 01:12:45,510
Most words are predictable, whereas image tokens

1159
01:12:45,510 --> 01:12:46,810
have very high entropy.

1160
01:12:46,810 --> 01:12:50,970
I don't know what shade of blue this exact token is going to be.

1161
01:12:50,970 --> 01:12:55,890
And so as a result, they found that training with this mixture

1162
01:12:55,890 --> 01:12:59,970
led to a lot of the norms of the parameters

1163
01:12:59,970 --> 01:13:05,890
to grow and to generate loss instability.

1164
01:13:05,890 --> 01:13:08,290
They were able to mitigate or fix this

1165
01:13:08,290 --> 01:13:12,810
to some extent via QK norm and z-loss regularization, which

1166
01:13:12,810 --> 01:13:16,870
controls the norm growth.

1167
01:13:16,870 --> 01:13:20,310
So I'm not even going to show the results.

1168
01:13:20,310 --> 01:13:24,210
But I wanted to highlight this work because there's

1169
01:13:24,210 --> 01:13:28,210
a certain elegance here because it's just one model that treats

1170
01:13:28,210 --> 01:13:31,330
really all modalities the same.

1171
01:13:31,330 --> 01:13:35,010
But the downside is that it turned out

1172
01:13:35,010 --> 01:13:38,210
this model was not really as performant,

1173
01:13:38,210 --> 01:13:42,890
and the discretization does definitely lose its information.

1174
01:13:42,890 --> 01:13:46,590
So think about OCR.

1175
01:13:46,590 --> 01:13:51,090
Again, if you discretize very small print,

1176
01:13:51,090 --> 01:13:54,030
you're not going to be able to read it anymore.

1177
01:13:54,030 --> 01:13:57,570
And finally, training with multi modalities is also tricky.

1178
01:13:57,570 --> 01:13:59,870
We saw this a little bit with the Qwen models

1179
01:13:59,870 --> 01:14:02,130
and we had to play around with the weights.

1180
01:14:02,130 --> 01:14:05,350
But this is more exacerbated here.

1181
01:14:05,350 --> 01:14:10,430
So VQ-VAEs were popular for a while.

1182
01:14:10,430 --> 01:14:15,690
And in fact, many people were using this for image generation.

1183
01:14:15,690 --> 01:14:19,525
The main reason to do that is, well, you have a transformer.

1184
01:14:19,525 --> 01:14:21,150
How do you generate from a transformer?

1185
01:14:21,150 --> 01:14:22,990
Well, you have to generate discrete things

1186
01:14:22,990 --> 01:14:28,510
and you basically put all your data into this discrete form.

1187
01:14:28,510 --> 01:14:32,230
But then diffusion models came out

1188
01:14:32,230 --> 01:14:35,410
and became popular and viable for generation.

1189
01:14:35,410 --> 01:14:43,070
So this flavor of method is less popular than it used to be.

1190
01:14:43,070 --> 01:14:48,560
OK, so maybe just reflect and summarize here.

1191
01:14:48,560 --> 01:14:50,920
Frontier models these days are expected

1192
01:14:50,920 --> 01:14:55,640
to be multimodal or even more strongly natively multimodal

1193
01:14:55,640 --> 01:14:58,000
or omni models.

1194
01:14:58,000 --> 01:15:01,560
Unfortunately, I think when Gemini comes out

1195
01:15:01,560 --> 01:15:04,440
or GPT comes out, they tout or touted

1196
01:15:04,440 --> 01:15:07,900
as being natively multimodal and handle all these modalities.

1197
01:15:07,900 --> 01:15:09,425
And in fact, they do.

1198
01:15:09,425 --> 01:15:14,240
But of course, there's no details about how these are built.

1199
01:15:14,240 --> 01:15:18,840
I think that it's probably combination

1200
01:15:18,840 --> 01:15:22,840
of having a continuous encoder because you don't want

1201
01:15:22,840 --> 01:15:26,400
to lose information and then diffusion for the generation

1202
01:15:26,400 --> 01:15:31,000
because-- but that's my speculation.

1203
01:15:31,000 --> 01:15:32,897
The fundamental challenge, I think,

1204
01:15:32,897 --> 01:15:34,480
when you're dealing with multimodality

1205
01:15:34,480 --> 01:15:39,360
is how do you handle non-text modalities?

1206
01:15:39,360 --> 01:15:40,920
It's also interesting that there's

1207
01:15:40,920 --> 01:15:44,260
a asymmetry between understanding the modality

1208
01:15:44,260 --> 01:15:45,540
and generation.

1209
01:15:45,540 --> 01:15:49,700
And there's no one universal encoder let's say.

1210
01:15:49,700 --> 01:15:53,060
For example, when we looked at CLIP,

1211
01:15:53,060 --> 01:15:55,300
you only cared for classification

1212
01:15:55,300 --> 01:15:57,080
to capture high level semantics.

1213
01:15:57,080 --> 01:15:59,920
So these vectors could be fairly small

1214
01:15:59,920 --> 01:16:02,380
and capture the high level semantics.

1215
01:16:02,380 --> 01:16:05,980
Whereas if you wanted to do OCR or whether you wanted

1216
01:16:05,980 --> 01:16:09,780
to generate an image, then you need really fine-grained detail.

1217
01:16:09,780 --> 01:16:12,740
You need to-- that's why diffusion is so good because it

1218
01:16:12,740 --> 01:16:20,673
can really micro optimize the low-high frequency information.

1219
01:16:20,673 --> 01:16:22,340
I think, in general, when you're dealing

1220
01:16:22,340 --> 01:16:26,020
with multiple modalities, you have to think carefully

1221
01:16:26,020 --> 01:16:30,820
about weighing them properly.

1222
01:16:30,820 --> 01:16:34,220
For example, video certainly has lower information density

1223
01:16:34,220 --> 01:16:35,740
than text.

1224
01:16:35,740 --> 01:16:41,590
So you don't want a video to overwhelm your text.

1225
01:16:41,590 --> 01:16:47,710
And like I said before, at least the current best thing

1226
01:16:47,710 --> 01:16:52,190
is having continuous encoders.

1227
01:16:52,190 --> 01:16:55,270
It seems even CLIP, even though it's five years old,

1228
01:16:55,270 --> 01:16:59,510
or similar ideas are still the go-to way

1229
01:16:59,510 --> 01:17:03,630
to capture semantics of images.

1230
01:17:03,630 --> 01:17:05,590
Transformers are still there.

1231
01:17:05,590 --> 01:17:08,350
And then diffusion models, which I didn't talk about,

1232
01:17:08,350 --> 01:17:12,910
are great for generation.

1233
01:17:12,910 --> 01:17:16,430
OK, so I will stop there.

1234
01:17:16,430 --> 01:17:22,870
That's it for the lecture on multimodality.

1235
01:17:22,870 --> 01:17:25,730
I guess we don't have any homework on doing this.

1236
01:17:25,730 --> 01:17:29,430
But if you're curious, I think I encourage

1237
01:17:29,430 --> 01:17:34,060
you to play around with training some of these models.
